// A polymorphic helper called at both a fixed type and a parameter-derived
// one, inside a function that is itself used at two types.
//
// This ran wrong, then refused, and now works; the file has outlived two
// expectations and is kept because the shape is easy to break again.
//
// It used to be emitted wrongly and in silence. `hold` was monomorphized
// into an `int` copy and a `str` copy; both declarations got the right
// signature, and both bodies got the `int` one's operations — a
// `mere_vec_str*` function calling `mere_vec_int_push` in the first version
// of this test, which used a vec. Not a wrong answer, no answer, and only
// when the C compiler saw it.
//
// The cause was `specialize_single_use_local_fns`, a codegen pass that
// fixes a local polymorphic fn's type in place when the body contains
// exactly one concrete use of it. `hold 1` is concrete and `hold v` is not
// — not yet, because it only resolves once `mk` is instantiated — so the
// pass read "one concrete arrow" as "used at one type" and unified the
// definition with `int`. The other instantiation then had nothing left to
// unify with. It now also requires that the body contain no unresolved use
// of the name, which is what `hold v` is.
//
// `hold` here allocates nothing, so the narrow value restriction, mutable
// containers and the region model are all out of the picture. Three
// neighbouring shapes always worked and still do: one instantiation of
// `mk`, `hold` called only at the parameter-derived type, and `hold` used
// directly at two types. It was the combination and nothing smaller.
//
// contrib/state/store was the program that found it — a store instantiated
// at two state types calls `cell_new` at a type derived from S and at plain
// `int` for its token counter — and it uses contrib/state/cell again now
// that this works.

let hold = fn (v) -> (v, v);

// `hold v` is parameter-derived; `hold 1` is fixed. Both in one body.
let mk = fn (v) -> (hold v, hold 1);

let (a1, a2) = mk 1 in
let (b1, b2) = mk "s" in
let _ = print (show (fst a1) ++ fst b1 ++ show (snd a2 + snd b2)) in
0
